Chapter: FireworksKernel
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceEffect
  cites Gpu chapter Thread

 The [Device] shader form of the bare-metal fireworks simulation. One GPU
 thread owns one spark. Each thread derives its spark's launch angle and
 speed from its global index, integrates the ballistic arc under gravity
 for the current frame, fades the color over the spark's life, and writes
 the packed pixel back to a global output buffer. Compiled through the PTX
 and SPIR-V plugs, the [Device]-effected kernels below become real GPU
 shader code -- the same physics the CPU preview runs, now per-thread.

 The kernel entry points take a trailing `gid` parameter: the PTX plug
 wraps each in a launch stub that computes the global thread id as
 thread-idx-x + block-idx-x * block-dim-x and dispatches one invocation
 per spark. Angles are milliradian-scaled (0..6283) and the trig helpers
 return 1/1000 fixed point, so all arithmetic stays integer and the GPU
 spark positions match the host preview exactly. The trig is pure device
 math; only the kernels that touch global memory carry the Device effect.

 We say:

Section: Fixed-Point Trig

  fw-wrap : Integer -> Integer
  fw-wrap (a) = let m = a - (a / 6283) * 6283 in if m < 0 then m + 6283 else m

  fw-sin-core : Integer -> Integer
  fw-sin-core (x) =
    let x2 = x * x / 1000
    in let x3 = x2 * x / 1000
    in let x5 = x3 * x2 / 1000
    in x - x3 / 6 + x5 / 120

  fw-sin : Integer -> Integer
  fw-sin (raw) =
    let a = fw-wrap raw
    in if a <= 1570 then fw-sin-core a
    else if a <= 3141 then fw-sin-core (3141 - a)
    else if a <= 4712 then 0 - fw-sin-core (a - 3141)
    else 0 - fw-sin-core (6283 - a)

  fw-cos : Integer -> Integer
  fw-cos (raw) = fw-sin (raw + 1570)

  fw-jit-speed : Integer -> Integer
  fw-jit-speed (gid) = 260 + (gid * 37 - (gid / 29) * 29 * 37)

Section: Spark Kernel

 fw-burst-spark: procedural burst. The spark index gives its ray angle
 and a jittered speed; the thread integrates x/y under gravity, fades the
 brightness over life, and packs (brightness, x, y) into one word stored
 at out[gid]. The trig is pure; the single global write is the tail.

  fw-burst-spark : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device] Integer
  fw-burst-spark (out) (cx) (cy) (frame) (nspark) (gid) =
    let ang = fw-wrap (gid * 6283 / nspark)
    in let spd = fw-jit-speed gid
    in let vx = fw-cos ang * spd / 1000
    in let vy = fw-sin ang * spd / 1000
    in let px = cx + vx * frame / 256
    in let py = cy + vy * frame / 256 + 20 * frame * frame / 512
    in let life = 200 - frame
    in let bright = if life < 0 then 0 else life * 255 / 200
    in let packed = bright * 16777216 + px * 4096 + py
    in device-store out gid packed

Section: Integrate Kernel

 fw-integrate: read-modify-write. Each thread loads its spark's four
 packed state words (x, y, vx, vy) from the state buffer, advances one
 frame of ballistic motion, and stores the new screen position to out.
 The four global reads are act-bound; the store is the tail.

  fw-integrate : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device] Integer
  fw-integrate (state) (out) (frame) (grav) (gid) = act
    px0 <- device-load state (gid * 4)
    py0 <- device-load state (gid * 4 + 1)
    vx <- device-load state (gid * 4 + 2)
    vy <- device-load state (gid * 4 + 3)
    let px = px0 + vx * frame / 256
    in let py = py0 + vy * frame / 256 + grav * frame * frame / 512
    in let packed = px * 65536 + py
    in device-store out gid packed
  end

